"use strict";(self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara=self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara||[]).push([["37416"],{92130(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"FIN-405","slug":"price-to-sales-ratio","term":"Price-to-Sales Ratio (P/S)","also_known_as":["P/S"],"difficulty":"Intermediate","definition":"The price-to-sales ratio (P/S) compares a company\'s market capitalization with its total revenue, showing how much investors are paying for each unit of sales.","explanation":["The P/S ratio is calculated by dividing market capitalization by annual revenue, or equivalently, share price by revenue per share.","For example, a company with a market capitalization of 3 billion and annual revenue of 1.5 billion has a P/S ratio of 2.","P/S is useful for valuing companies that are not yet profitable, such as young, fast-growing businesses, because price-to-earnings ratios cannot be used when earnings are negative. Revenue is also harder to manipulate through accounting choices than earnings.","Its major limitation is that it ignores profitability. A company with high sales but very thin or negative profit margins may not deserve a high P/S ratio. Two companies with the same revenue can have very different values if one earns a 25% margin and the other loses money. P/S also ignores debt, which is why some analysts prefer EV/Revenue. P/S ratios are most meaningful when comparing companies in the same industry with similar margins."],"note":"","total":1500,"prev":{"slug":"price-to-free-cash-flow-ratio","term":"Price-to-Free-Cash-Flow Ratio"},"next":{"slug":"price-volume-mix-analysis","term":"Price-Volume-Mix Analysis"}}')}}]);